{"name":"English Channel Fog","hex":"#cbd3e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"english","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Spinning or rotary motion given to a ball around the vertical axis, as in pool, billiards or bowling; spin, sidespin.","An unusual or unexpected interpretation of a text or idea, a spin, a nuance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of English."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/english"},{"term":"channel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hollow bed of running waters;  the bed of the sea or other body of water.","The natural or man-made deeper course through a reef, bar, bay, or any shallow body of water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or cut a channel or groove in.","To direct or guide along a desired course."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/channel"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"English Channel","blocks":[{"text":"The English Channel, also known as the Channel, is an arm of the Atlantic Ocean that separates Southern England from northern France. It links to the southern part of the North Sea by the Strait of Dover at its northeastern end. It is the busiest shipping area in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/English_Channel_Satellite.jpg/330px-English_Channel_Satellite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Channel","wikidata":"Q34640"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg/500px-Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg/500px-Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Osborne House, the summer retreat of Queen Victoria on the Isle of Wight. Starting from the late 18th century, settlements on and around the English Channel coastline in England grew rapidly into thriving seaside resorts, bolstered by their association with royalty and the middle and upper classes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOsborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Carte_de_la_Manche.png/500px-Carte_de_la_Manche.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Carte_de_la_Manche.png/500px-Carte_de_la_Manche.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of the channel area with French nomenclature","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACar